Natural selection can be simulated using MATLAB by modeling a simple population with different traits and their respective fitness values. Here's an example:

```matlab
% Define population size and initial traits
population_size = 100;
traits = [0.5 0.7 0.3 0.9 0.1]; % Traits ranging from 0 to 1

% Define fitness function based on traits
fitness = @(x) x(1) * x(2) * x(3) * x(4); % Fitness is a product of all traits

% Simulate natural selection for 10 generations
for i = 1:10
    % Evaluate fitness of each individual
    fitness_values = fitness(traits);

    % Select parents based on fitness
    parents = sort(fitness_values, 'descend');
    parent_indices = parents(1:2); % Choose two parents with highest fitness

    % Create offspring by combining traits of parents
    offspring = zeros(2, length(traits));
    for j = 1:length(traits)
        offspring(1, j) = traits(parent_indices(1), j);
        offspring(2, j) = traits(parent_indices(2), j);
    end

    % Update population with offspring
    traits = offspring;
end

% Display final population and average fitness
disp(['Final population: ', num2str(traits)]);
disp(['Average fitness: ', num2str(mean(fitness(traits)))]);
```

In this simulation, the population starts with random traits, and the fitness of each individual is evaluated based on the product of their traits. Parents are selected based on their fitness, and offspring are created by combining the traits of the parents. The population then evolves over 10 generations, with the fittest individuals passing on their traits to the next generation. The final population and average fitness are displayed after the simulation.

This example demonstrates how natural selection can lead to the evolution of a population towards individuals with higher fitness, as those with more advantageous traits are more likely to survive and reproduce.

