Here's a decorator for Nest.js middleware that checks the `NODE_ENV` variable and calls `next()` if it's set to 'true', but returns `false` if it's set to 'false':

```typescript
import { Injectable, CanActivate, ExecutionContext } from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class EnvironmentGuard implements CanActivate {
  canActivate(context: ExecutionContext): boolean {
    const request = context.switchToHttp().getRequest();
    const nodeEnv = process.env.NODE_ENV;

    if (nodeEnv === 'true') {
      return true;
    } else if (nodeEnv === 'false') {
      return false;
    } else {
      throw new Error('Invalid NODE_ENV value');
    }
  }
}
```

You can use this guard in your controller or route like this:

```typescript
import { Controller, Get, UseGuards } from '@nestjs/common';
import { EnvironmentGuard } from './environment.guard';

@Controller('api')
export class MyController {
  @Get()
  @UseGuards(EnvironmentGuard)
  getHello(): string {
    return 'Hello World!';
  }
}
```

In this example, the `getHello` method will only be accessible if the `NODE_ENV` variable is set to 'true'. If it's set to 'false', the method will return `false`.

